// A genuine deadlock: two tasks that each join the other, so once both have
// parked nothing can ever wake either one -- the exact shape the executor's
// deadlock check is sound for (`crates/nova-runtime/src/task.rs`'s
// `run_to_completion`: an empty ready queue with a non-empty park set and no
// `Wait::Deadline` means every remaining wait is a `Task(_)` whose target is
// itself parked, so nothing can complete and nothing can wake).
//
// This is deliberately NOT "spawn a task that loops forever via `yield_now`
// and join it" -- that shape was tried first and reverted. A `while true {
// yield_now().await }` task re-queues itself every turn forever, which means
// the ready queue is NEVER truly empty, which means `run_to_completion`'s
// outer deadlock check (gated on the inner queue-drain loop actually
// exiting) is never reached at all -- a livelock, not a deadlock, and
// indistinguishable from one by any sound check (the two are the halting
// problem apart: telling "never completes" from "hasn't completed yet"
// requires waiting an unbounded time). Confirmed by hand: that version hangs
// under a busy CPU spin whether `join` spins or parks, so it did not even
// discriminate the thing it was meant to prove. See task-3-report.md for
// the trace and for the design spec passage (3.3) that independently
// predicts exactly this: "`spawn(forever_yielding())` alongside
// `sleep(10).await` would hang for good" -- the same mechanism, one task
// earlier.
//
// The cycle below has no such task: `a` and `b` each park on the other
// exactly once, on their first and only poll, so the queue drains for real.
// Building it needs a rendezvous, because `waiter(target)` must name its
// *peer's* handle, and neither handle exists before both tasks are spawned.
// `JoinHandle` is a plain public-field record (ADR 0007) with reference
// semantics (ADR 0005), so a `mut`-bound handle works as that rendezvous
// slot: each `waiter` is called with a placeholder handle, and the
// placeholder's `fut` field is overwritten with the real peer handle after
// both tasks are spawned -- entirely within `setup`'s own first poll, before
// either spawned task has had a chance to run, so there is no ordering
// hazard to reason about.
//
// If this regresses, it manifests as a HANG, not a failed assertion: this
// is a `nova run` gate (not `nova test`), and `nova test` itself has no
// per-test timeout either (known debt), so nothing here times out on its
// own. Kill the process and reason about the runtime's queue/park-set state
// rather than assuming the fixture is wrong -- the Rust-level unit tests in
// `crates/nova-runtime/src/task.rs`
// (`joining_a_pending_task_parks_and_is_woken_when_the_target_finishes` and
// `a_deadlock_names_every_parked_task_and_its_reason`) exist precisely to
// give a fast, non-hanging signal for the same mechanism.
async fn waiter<T>(handle: JoinHandle<T>) -> T {
    handle.join().await
}

fn main() {
    block_on(setup())
}

async fn setup() {
    let mut handle_for_a = JoinHandle { fut: yield_now() }
    let mut handle_for_b = JoinHandle { fut: yield_now() }
    let fut_a = waiter(handle_for_b)
    let fut_b = waiter(handle_for_a)
    let ha = spawn(fut_a)
    let hb = spawn(fut_b)
    handle_for_a.fut = ha.fut
    handle_for_b.fut = hb.fut
}
